{
  "teamId": 117,
  "year": 2023,
  "narrative": "The 2023 Houston Astros arrived as defending World Series champions, and from the first swings of the season, they carried both the weight of expectation and the talent to meet it. Opening weekend brought an early scare — Andrew Vaughn's two-run double off Ryan Pressly on Opening Day stung — but Yordan Alvarez answered the very next night with a clutch two-run double that set the tone for what this lineup was capable of. New addition José Abreu delivered immediately as well, and the Astros settled into their familiar role as one of baseball's most dangerous offenses, posting a 14-12 April before catching fire in May and July.\n\nThe heart of the season was defined by explosive individual moments from the core. Altuve's grand slam on May 29, Tucker's walk-off grand slam in August, Bregman's towering two-run shot in July — each came at high-leverage moments and kept Houston's head above water through the inevitable turbulence. But the turbulence was real. June was a bruising month, with Ryan Pressly and the bullpen suffering multiple meltdowns, including a brutal three-game Cleveland series on June 9 that saw Seth Martinez and Pressly each surrender crucial hits. The Astros finished June 13-14, their only losing month of the summer, before bouncing back with a strong July and August.\n\nSeptember told a harder story. Pressly's struggles continued to mount, culminating in a devastating three-run home run to Cedric Mullins on September 18 that carried a -0.634 WPA — the single most damaging moment of the regular season. The Astros stumbled to a 12-14 September, finishing 90-72 and entering October without the swagger of a juggernaut, but with enough firepower to believe.\n\nThe postseason brought flashes of brilliance and ultimate heartbreak. Abreu's home runs in both the ALDS and ALCS showed he could deliver on the biggest stages, and Altuve's three-run blast in Game 6 of the ALCS momentarily threatened a miraculous comeback. But the Texas Rangers, riding Adolis García and timely pitching, proved too much. Pressly's shaky outings followed the team into October, and in the end Houston's season closed not with a championship coronation but with the sting of an ALCS exit — a 90-win club that never quite recaptured the dominance of years past.",
